    <h2>The chapter ending: what is actually on screen<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>This is the part of the page we rewound the most, because Parking is room 26 of 26 and "last level" usually means a send-off. Here is exactly what the footage shows.</p>
    <p>Pressing <b>P</b> plays a short lift ride, the doors open on the top parking level, and the results panel comes up. It is the same panel the game uses for every room: <b>LEVEL COMPLETED!</b>, a trophy, <b>Reward +50</b>, five stars, a ten-block Efficiency bar at ten of ten, and <b>Time: 6:13</b>. Under it, the piggy-bank strip reads <b>200</b> and there is a <b>Claim ×2</b> button. There is no chapter title card, no "Chapter 2 complete" banner, no cutscene of the thieves, no letter, no newspaper, and no next-level button in the panel itself. The second recording ends on the same card, with the same layout; only its clock differs.</p>
    <p>So the honest answer to "what happens at the end of Chapter 2" is: the level ends, the chapter does not announce itself. If there is an epilogue, it plays after the results panel — behind the Claim button or on a continue prompt that neither recording presses — and we cannot describe it. What we can say is that the room count is 26, Parking is 26, and the results card after Parking is a normal results card.</p>
    <p>That is worth knowing before you play it, because it changes how you read the level. Parking is not a finale in structure: it is a bigger, two-floor room that reuses every idea Chapter 2 has taught — an item gate, a sequence panel, a locator board, a plate puzzle, a fader panel — and then stops. The story beat, if the game has one, is not in this room.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>The hatch does not respond.</b> Check the item, not the hatch. The paperclip has to be examined into a <b>Modified paperclip</b> before the keyhole will take it. This is the single most common place to stall, because the clip is already in the bag and nothing on screen tells you to examine it.</p>
    <p><b>The button bank resets.</b> The bank keeps its green lights between presses, but the pattern is order-sensitive. If you have lit most of the panel and the doors still will not open, you are not missing a button — you pressed two of them in the wrong order somewhere. Start the pattern again from the first button rather than hunting for an unlit one.</p>
    <p><b>The twelve-button panel will not accept a row.</b> Each row is four digits and there is no screen: the buttons themselves display the digits. If a row will not settle, check the digit you are setting against the plate, not against the plaque's line breaks. The plaque puts "parking: 1876" on its own line and "H:" two lines above "2002", and reading it as a single column of numbers is the classic mistake.</p>
    <p><b>3 or 8 on the G1 plate.</b> The seven-segment 3 is open on the left; an 8 is closed. At the distance the camera sits, the difference is a few pixels. 3542 is the reading that survives both recordings and the plaque, so use it as the tie-breaker if you are unsure.</p>
    <p><b>P stays dark.</b> That means the switch on -1 did not take, or the keypad reset when you left the box. Go back, re-check that the display still reads <b>0243</b>, and throw the switch again before riding up.</p>
    <p><b>The fader lamps will not all go green.</b> Work one fader at a time and watch its own lamp. The panel gives no other feedback, and the car silhouettes on the left edge are decoration — they do not encode the heights.</p>



    <h2>Where Parking sits in Chapter 2<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 2 is <i>No Honor Among Thieves</i>, and its twenty-six rooms run through a town's worth of small businesses before they end in this car park: sports store, bookstore, furniture store, household appliances, print shop, bakery, toy store, restroom, and then Parking. The last stretch is worth keeping in mind because Parking reuses their grammar — a picked lock, a sequence panel, a plate puzzle, a fader board — without reusing their props. Nothing you carried out of the toy store or the restroom follows you in here; Parking starts you with an empty bag in a lift.</p>
    <p>If you are working through the chapter in order, the two rooms immediately before this one are <b>24 Toy Store</b> and <b>25 Restroom</b>. Parking is a step up in size from both: two floors instead of one, two independent code systems instead of one, and a lift you will ride a dozen times. Budget ten to fifteen minutes for a first run if you read the clues yourself, and six if you follow the route above.</p>

